  "query_preview": "..  (Friday Night through Next Thursday)\nIssued at 246 PM CDT Thu May 14 2026\n\nAfter a relatively drier period of conditions Friday evening into \nearly overnight, CAMs indicate another period of more numerous \nshowers and thunderstorms Saturday morning into the afternoon as \nanother nocturnal LLJ veers into the CWA and one or more \nperturbations in upper-level quasi-zonal flow pass. Given the subtle \ndrivers of showers and thunderstorms, their exact coverage, timing, \nand location are not completely clear through the day with notable \ndifferences across CAM solutions. Deep-layer wind shear will be \nweaker (generally 15 to 25 kt) than Friday but instability is \nanticipated to be higher, leading to a less organized pulse or \nmulticell nature to thunderstorms. This environment, however, may \nsupport a few microbursts that produce gusty winds, but confidence \nis low that thunderstorms will become severe at this time. It is \nworth considering that these patterns can sometimes yield MCVs that \nlead to locally higher wind shear. With the varied depictions of \nclouds and precipitation on Saturday, confidence is not high the \nhigh temperature forecast with ensemble model interquartile ranges \nspanning the mid-70s to mid-80s F, which has actually grown in the \nlast 24 hours.\n\nProbabilities of measurable rainfall in ensemble model guidance \ngradually decrease/lift northward Saturday night into Sunday morning \nas the nose of another nocturnal LLJ is focused more northward than \nprevious nights and mid-levels heights rise again with the upper-\nlevel pattern amplifying across the CONUS. With height rises \nremaining dominant into Sunday, predominantly dry conditions are \nanticipated along with more confidence in warm temperatures. The \ncurrent forecast, influenced by the very warm, bias-corrected NBM, \nreflects the warm end of the ensemble model envelope with the most \nsupport for high temperatures in the 80s to around 90 F. This \nremains the case into early next week while the CWA resides in the \nwarm sector.\n\nUpper-level troughing will take place Sunday into early Monday over \nthe Rocky Mountains, but global model guidance continues to vary in \nthe track and timing of an initial shortwave trough ejection on \nMonday, determining the eastward progression of an approaching cold \nfront. Namely, the variability is centered around whether or not the \nfront more quickly passes through the CWA late Monday or more slowly \nlate Tuesday or Wednesday as additional, subtle shortwave troughs \nnudge the front eastward. The timing of this cold front will be \nimportant in both determining when chances of showers and \nthunderstorms peak early next week as well as contextualizing the \nsevere threat across the CWA. WPC cluster analyses applied to \nensemble model guidance have slightly higher membership with a \nslower frontal passage, which is also supported by machine learning \nguidance probabilities of severe weather being higher to the west of \nthe CWA on Monday. The overall magnitude of severe weather may be \nrelatively lower after Monday as well, with deep-layer wind shear \nand large-scale forcing advertised to be weaker on Tuesday and \nWednesday regardless of frontal position. Conversely, a slower \nmoving front and the potential for repeated showers and \nthunderstorms would conceptually bear watching for heavy rainfall. \nAll of these factors contribute to lower confidence in the details \nof severe weather early next week, but the pattern is favorable \nenough for messaging severe weather at this longer than average lead \ntime.\n\nWhenever the front finally passes through the CWA, more seasonable \ntemperatures and a period of dry conditions will ensue mid-next\nweek.\n\nPfahler",
